<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/logic-gates-and-digital-circuits">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between the binary numbers lesson and this one sits the physical miracle: <strong>bits are
    voltages, and logic gates are transistors arranged to compute</strong>. AND, OR, NOT and
    their cousins are tiny circuits that read inputs as high/low voltages and output another.
    Claude Shannon proved in 1937 that these gates implement Boolean algebra — and the digital
    age followed from a master's thesis.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five gates to know — ending with the one that builds everything" widget="equationStep" config={gateCfg} />

  <h2>From gates to computers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Half adder:</strong> XOR gives the sum digit, AND gives the carry — one bit of arithmetic. Chain full adders and you can add numbers of any size.</li>
    <li><strong>Memory is feedback:</strong> gate a gate's output back into its input and it <em>remembers</em> — a latch. Billions of latches, refined into registers and RAM.</li>
    <li><strong>Clock:</strong> a circuit that flips billions of times per second synchronises everything; "3.2 GHz" is its tempo.</li>
    <li><strong>Abstraction stack:</strong> transistors → gates → adders/registers → processor → machine code → your app. Each layer hides the one below — the defining idea of computer engineering.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Boole (1854); Shannon (1937); Petzold, Code; Neso Academy digital logic series.</p>
</TopicPage>
